{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JKPKByXAs58kn2FVBNDXPQ","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"liquid lithium self-diffusion coefficient temperature dependence experimental NMR Murday Cotts\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Structural and transport properties of LiTFSI/G3 electrolyte with machine-learned molecular dynamics\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2503.20243\"},{\"title\":\"Helium bubbles in liquid lithium: a potential issue for ITER\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2304.00293\"},{\"title\":\"self diffusion coefficient study of liquid lithium\",\"url\":\"https://scholar.xjtu.edu.cn/en/publications/self-diffusion-coefficient-study-of-liquid-lithium/\"},{\"title\":\"s00231 011 0874 9\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00231-011-0874-9\"},{\"title\":\"link.springer.com\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF00883553\"},{\"title\":\"Self-diffusion in liquid lithium from coherent quasielastic neutron scattering\",\"url\":\"https://katalog.lib.cas.cz/KNAV/EdsRecord/edsair,edsair.doi...........2430b859cd2c8ede718b24c249f43fd0\"},{\"title\":\"peeref.com\",\"url\":\"https://peeref.com/works/14514882\"},{\"title\":\"m.math-net.ru\",\"url\":\"https://m.math-net.ru/eng/ftt11863\"},{\"title\":\"m.math-net.ru\",\"url\":\"https://m.math-net.ru/eng/tvt693\"},{\"title\":\"subject:Self diffusion coefficients\",\"url\":\"https://fitforthem.unipa.it/subject:Self-diffusion coefficients\"}]\n\nThe search did not turn up the Murday and Cotts data itself, but it did confirm the paper and some later work that compares against it.\n\n- **The original paper:** Murday and Cotts published \"Self-diffusion coefficient of liquid lithium\" in J. Chem. Phys. 48 (1968), pp. 4938–4945. This appears only in reference lists, so I could not retrieve its fitted temperature dependence or exact values. Murday JS, Cotts RM (1968) Self-diffusion coefficient of liquid lithium. J Chem Phys 48:4938–4945\n- **Later simulation work:** A 2012 study using an embedded-atom potential reports that the liquid lithium self-diffusion coefficient increases with temperature and decreases with pressure. It says its calculated values are in good agreement with Murday's experiment results in atmosphere, and it derives an Arrhenius form from its simulations.\n- **Neutron scattering:** A 2014 study measured the temperature dependence of liquid lithium self-diffusion from 500–830 K using coherent quasi-elastic neutron scattering. The temperature dependence of the self-diffusion coefficient of liquid lithium in the temperature range of 500–830 K has been investigated using coherent quasi-elastic neutron scattering.\n- **Helium-bubble simulations:** A 2023 molecular dynamics study of lithium with helium inclusions reports diffusion values that at temperatures of 470 and 843 K are in overall good agreement with the available experimental ones it cites.\n- **Solid-lithium NMR:** A separate NMR study of ⁷Li spin relaxation in solid lithium fits a self-diffusion expression of the form D = D₁₀·exp(−Q₁/RT)·[1 + D₂₁·exp(−Q₂₁/RT)], with Q₁ ≈ 12.0 kcal/mol. This is for the solid phase, not the liquid. The result for the self-diffusion coefficient D SD is given by D SD = D 10·exp(−Q 1/RT)·[1+D 21·exp(−Q 21/RT)].\n\nFor the actual Murday–Cotts numbers, you would need the 1968 paper itself, which these results do not include.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a68a7c77-53eb-48b5-97ba-747962629b47","uuid":"7ef1a9fa-a3a3-48ef-9814-d4b313e0ca8e","timestamp":"2026-11-01T07:39:08.565Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"liquid lithium self-diffusion coefficient temperature dependence experimental NMR Murday Cotts","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01UydYtxo9JJKDusJGyieoUV","content":[{"title":"Structural and transport properties of LiTFSI/G3 electrolyte with machine-learned molecular dynamics","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2503.20243"},{"title":"Helium bubbles in liquid lithium: a potential issue for ITER","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2304.00293"},{"title":"self diffusion coefficient study of liquid lithium","url":"https://scholar.xjtu.edu.cn/en/publications/self-diffusion-coefficient-study-of-liquid-lithium/"},{"title":"s00231 011 0874 9","url":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00231-011-0874-9"},{"title":"link.springer.com","url":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF00883553"},{"title":"Self-diffusion in liquid lithium from coherent quasielastic neutron scattering","url":"https://katalog.lib.cas.cz/KNAV/EdsRecord/edsair,edsair.doi...........2430b859cd2c8ede718b24c249f43fd0"},{"title":"peeref.com","url":"https://peeref.com/works/14514882"},{"title":"m.math-net.ru","url":"https://m.math-net.ru/eng/ftt11863"},{"title":"m.math-net.ru","url":"https://m.math-net.ru/eng/tvt693"},{"title":"subject:Self diffusion coefficients","url":"https://fitforthem.unipa.it/subject:Self-diffusion coefficients"}]},"The search did not turn up the Murday and Cotts data itself, but it did confirm the paper and some later work that compares against it.\n\n- **The original paper:** Murday and Cotts published \"Self-diffusion coefficient of liquid lithium\" in J. Chem. Phys. 48 (1968), pp. 4938–4945. This appears only in reference lists, so I could not retrieve its fitted temperature dependence or exact values. Murday JS, Cotts RM (1968) Self-diffusion coefficient of liquid lithium. J Chem Phys 48:4938–4945\n- **Later simulation work:** A 2012 study using an embedded-atom potential reports that the liquid lithium self-diffusion coefficient increases with temperature and decreases with pressure. It says its calculated values are in good agreement with Murday's experiment results in atmosphere, and it derives an Arrhenius form from its simulations.\n- **Neutron scattering:** A 2014 study measured the temperature dependence of liquid lithium self-diffusion from 500–830 K using coherent quasi-elastic neutron scattering. The temperature dependence of the self-diffusion coefficient of liquid lithium in the temperature range of 500–830 K has been investigated using coherent quasi-elastic neutron scattering.\n- **Helium-bubble simulations:** A 2023 molecular dynamics study of lithium with helium inclusions reports diffusion values that at temperatures of 470 and 843 K are in overall good agreement with the available experimental ones it cites.\n- **Solid-lithium NMR:** A separate NMR study of ⁷Li spin relaxation in solid lithium fits a self-diffusion expression of the form D = D₁₀·exp(−Q₁/RT)·[1 + D₂₁·exp(−Q₂₁/RT)], with Q₁ ≈ 12.0 kcal/mol. This is for the solid phase, not the liquid. The result for the self-diffusion coefficient D SD is given by D SD = D 10·exp(−Q 1/RT)·[1+D 21·exp(−Q 21/RT)].\n\nFor the actual Murday–Cotts numbers, you would need the 1968 paper itself, which these results do not include."],"durationSeconds":6.3509880239999985,"searchCount":1},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01JKPKByXAs58kn2FVBNDXPQ","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BScYyTH6iUwc24uWP8SBd5","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"liquid lithium density temperature correlation g/cm3 melting point 453.7 K\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"ntrs.nasa.gov\",\"url\":\"https://ntrs.nasa.gov/api/citations/19680018893/downloads/19680018893.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"repo.qst.go.jp\",\"url\":\"https://repo.qst.go.jp/records/47902\"},{\"title\":\"LITHIUM (Li)\",\"url\":\"https://qedfusion.org/LIB/PROPS/PANOS/li.html\"},{\"title\":\"repo.qst.go.jp\",\"url\":\"https://repo.qst.go.jp/records/47902/export/json\"},{\"title\":\"wulixb.iphy.ac.cn\",\"url\":\"https://wulixb.iphy.ac.cn/article/doi/10.7498/aps.55.76\"},{\"title\":\"Operational conditions:\",\"url\":\"https://www.chemeo.com/mid/22-277-a/Choline-chloride_Urea_lithium-chloride\"},{\"title\":\"Lithium chloride · LiCl\",\"url\":\"https://processconvert.pages.dev/substances/lithium-chloride\"},{\"title\":\"densite et temperature de cristallisation d une solution aqueuse de 22391\",\"url\":\"https://iifiir.org/fr/fridoc/densite-et-temperature-de-cristallisation-d-une-solution-aqueuse-de-22391\"},{\"title\":\"Contents - Index\",\"url\":\"https://fchartsoftware.com/ees/libr_help/hs400.htm\"},{\"title\":\"elogim.com\",\"url\":\"https://springerlink.bibliotecabuap.elogim.com/content/pdf/10.1361/105497198770341761.pdf\"}]\n\nThe search found one usable density correlation, but its temperature units are unstated, so verify before using it.\n\n- **Correlation (qedfusion.org):** rho = 0.5082 - 1.0336x10-4 T* - 4.8279x10-10 T*2 - 5.2853x10-12 T*3 (1) with T* = (T - 271.7), with density in g/cm³. The source does not say whether T is in K or °C.\n- **Melting point:** The same page lists Melting Point : 180.5 C, which equals about 453.7 K.\n- **Units check (my calculation, not from a source):** The offset 271.7 fits a °C input better than K. At 180.5 °C the formula gives about 0.518 g/cm³. At 453.7 K it gives about 0.489 g/cm³, which is noticeably low for liquid lithium near its melting point. So the formula most likely expects °C, but confirm this against the original reference.\n- **Alternative (NASA, 1968):** A NASA report correlated liquid lithium properties against temperature from experimental data, using a linear form of the polynomial to represent the density, and extrapolated to about 1600 K. I did not retrieve its coefficients, so I cannot give its values.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a68a7c77-53eb-48b5-97ba-747962629b47","uuid":"65ede598-86f5-40bd-9d9a-254cb360cd5b","timestamp":"2026-11-01T07:39:12.074Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"liquid lithium density temperature correlation g/cm3 melting point 453.7 K","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_0144v9PUwZp2avd5vEkxW7C7","content":[{"title":"ntrs.nasa.gov","url":"https://ntrs.nasa.gov/api/citations/19680018893/downloads/19680018893.pdf"},{"title":"repo.qst.go.jp","url":"https://repo.qst.go.jp/records/47902"},{"title":"LITHIUM (Li)","url":"https://qedfusion.org/LIB/PROPS/PANOS/li.html"},{"title":"repo.qst.go.jp","url":"https://repo.qst.go.jp/records/47902/export/json"},{"title":"wulixb.iphy.ac.cn","url":"https://wulixb.iphy.ac.cn/article/doi/10.7498/aps.55.76"},{"title":"Operational conditions:","url":"https://www.chemeo.com/mid/22-277-a/Choline-chloride_Urea_lithium-chloride"},{"title":"Lithium chloride · LiCl","url":"https://processconvert.pages.dev/substances/lithium-chloride"},{"title":"densite et temperature de cristallisation d une solution aqueuse de 22391","url":"https://iifiir.org/fr/fridoc/densite-et-temperature-de-cristallisation-d-une-solution-aqueuse-de-22391"},{"title":"Contents - Index","url":"https://fchartsoftware.com/ees/libr_help/hs400.htm"},{"title":"elogim.com","url":"https://springerlink.bibliotecabuap.elogim.com/content/pdf/10.1361/105497198770341761.pdf"}]},"The search found one usable density correlation, but its temperature units are unstated, so verify before using it.\n\n- **Correlation (qedfusion.org):** rho = 0.5082 - 1.0336x10-4 T* - 4.8279x10-10 T*2 - 5.2853x10-12 T*3 (1) with T* = (T - 271.7), with density in g/cm³. The source does not say whether T is in K or °C.\n- **Melting point:** The same page lists Melting Point : 180.5 C, which equals about 453.7 K.\n- **Units check (my calculation, not from a source):** The offset 271.7 fits a °C input better than K. At 180.5 °C the formula gives about 0.518 g/cm³. At 453.7 K it gives about 0.489 g/cm³, which is noticeably low for liquid lithium near its melting point. So the formula most likely expects °C, but confirm this against the original reference.\n- **Alternative (NASA, 1968):** A NASA report correlated liquid lithium properties against temperature from experimental data, using a linear form of the polynomial to represent the density, and extrapolated to about 1600 K. I did not retrieve its coefficients, so I cannot give its values."],"durationSeconds":9.320819942000002,"searchCount":1},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01BScYyTH6iUwc24uWP8SBd5","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
